Rudimentary executables are completed for the machines. This includes the teknic motors,
bath, temperature controller, and labjack. The binaries are written to the
    Mathematica-C-Integration\c++\Local\bin folder but I am copying and pasting them to the
    Mathematica-C-Integration\binaries folder which is the official folder for them to reduce clutter.

The important inputs are sent through the command line and received in the code with:

    int main(int argc, char** argv)

This way we can pass values from Mathematica to the executables.
An example command is:
    bath_set_temp.exe COM5 27.0
This sends the command to set bath temperature on COM5.

The outputs are sent through stdout so they can be read back into Mathematica.

The return values correspond with the success of the operation, with 0 meaning successful and any other number meaning unsuccessful.
So far, the binaries only need sFoundation20.dll to be in the same folder (to work with the motors). The bath, temp controller, labjack will work on their own.





Building these executables:

I wrote classes for the neslab bath and the temperature controller and edited the tasks.json file to produce this command when the run button is hit.

cmd /c chcp 65001>nul && cmd /c "call "C:\Users\Student\Documents\Summer 2025\Mathematica-C-Integration\c++\Local\.vscode\init_teknic_env.cmd" && cl.exe /Zi /EHsc /nologo /Fe"C:\Users\Student\Documents\Summer 2025\Mathematica-C-Integration\c++\Local\bin\bath_get_temp.exe" "C:\Users\Student\Documents\Summer 2025\Mathematica-C-Integration\c++\Local\bath_get_temp.cpp" classes\RTE7.cpp classes\Oven5R6900.cpp sFoundation20.lib"

This lets the compiler resolve the class references.